1. Figma MCP Server — AI-Assisted Design-to-Code
https://help.figma.com/hc/en-us/articles/32132100833559-Guide-to-the-Figma-MCP-server

The Figma MCP Server connects Figma designs directly to AI coding tools such as VS Code, Cursor, Claude Code, and Codex. 
Instead of giving an AI model only a screenshot, MCP provides structured information from the Figma file, including components, 
layout information, variables, colors, spacing, and other design details. This could allow our team to take completed Figma 
screens and use AI to generate an initial implementation that more closely follows the original design. It could be especially useful 
for quickly building responsive interfaces while still allowing developers to review and improve the generated code.

2. Anima — Convert Figma Designs into Frontend Code
https://docs.animaapp.com/docs/dev-mode

Anima is a Figma integration that automatically converts designs into responsive frontend code. It supports technologies such as 
React, Vue, HTML, CSS, and Tailwind and can translate Figma Auto Layout into responsive web layouts. For our project, Anima could be
used to quickly turn a completed wireframe or interface into an initial frontend implementation. Rather than manually recreating every 
button, card, form, and layout from Figma, the team could generate a starting point and then modify the resulting code to match 
our architecture and functionality requirements.

3. Figma for VS Code — Developer Handoff Inside the Code Editor
https://help.figma.com/hc/en-us/articles/15023121296151-Figma-for-VS-Code

Figma for VS Code brings Figma's developer tools directly into Visual Studio Code. Developers can inspect designs, view measurements 
and styles, access assets, read comments, examine component properties, and reference the design without constantly switching between 
Figma and the code editor. For our project, this could make the transition from wireframes to actual implementation much easier. 
A designer can prepare a finished screen in Figma while the developer can view the exact specifications next to the code they are writing, 
reducing misunderstandings between the design and implementation.

4. Maze + Figma — Test Our Prototype with Real Users Before Coding It
https://help.maze.co/articles/1286349424-importing-a-figma-prototype

Maze allows interactive Figma prototypes to be turned into usability tests. Users can be given tasks to complete inside the prototype, 
allowing the team to see whether navigation, buttons, forms, and application flows are intuitive before those features are fully implemented. 
This could be particularly useful for CSE442 because we can validate important user stories and application workflows before 
spending significant development time coding them. If users struggle with a workflow in the prototype, we can redesign it in Figma 
first rather than discovering the problem after implementing the feature.

5. Figma REST API — Build Custom Automation Around Our Designs
https://developers.figma.com/docs/rest-api/

Figma's REST API allows developers to programmatically access information stored inside Figma files, including frames, components, styles, 
comments, versions, images, and other design data. This opens the possibility of building our own tools around Figma rather than only using 
existing plugins. For example, our team could create a script that checks whether every feature has both desktop and mobile wireframes, 
verifies naming conventions, connects Figma screens to GitHub user stories, or tracks design changes between sprints. This would allow 
Figma to become part of our software-development workflow rather than simply being a tool for drawing interfaces.
